  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO \nCOUNTIES...\n\nThe thin marine layer gave it one last try this morning, with foggy \nconditions within 5 miles of the coastline. High pressure aloft will \nsubtly strengthen into the weekend, causing the marine layer to thin \neven further with mainly clear skies going forward each night. \nClockwise flow around the high pressure system to our northwest has \nallowed Santa Ana winds to move into the area. We have already \ngusted over 50 mph in some spots this morning across the San Diego \nCounty Mountains. Locally gusty conditions will continue this \nafternoon and overnight, before strengthening further late Saturday \nmorning and afternoon. Model guidance shows a fairly good chance (60-\n65%) chance of far inland valley locations like Ramona gust to 35 \nMPH. Since this wind will be mainly easterly, the strongest winds \nwill once again be across the San Diego Co mts and inland valleys, \nas well as the Inland Empire. Winds become weaker by Sunday in these \nareas with gusts closer to 20-25 MPH. Gusty winds and lower relative \nhumidity each day will lead to elevated to near-critical fire \nweather conditions, so make sure to do your part that leads to one \nless spark!\n\nThe main story will be the heat wave that is expected for areas west \nof the mountains. Much warmer weather is already occurring today \nwith highs 10-15 degrees warmer than yesterday. The area of high \npressure over the Desert Southwest will not move very much, with \nslight fluctuations in intensity. This, combined with increased \nSanta Ana winds on Saturday, will most make this day to be the \nhottest day of the event. Those within about 10 miles or more of the \ncoast have a 60-90% chance of seeing triple digit heat on Saturday, \nsimilar chances on Sunday. Always a tricky forecast with an offshore \nwinds pattern, where locations that do see an elevated east or \nnortheast winds will be hotter than those who do not. The same will \ngo for overnight temperatures as light offshore winds and a warm air \nmass will keep lower temperatures well into the 70s for areas west \nof the mountains. Other locations across the mountains and deserts \nwill still see highs 5 to 10 degrees above normal.\n\nThe area of high pressure may weaken slightly by the first half of \nnext week, but changes in temperatures will be rather minimal. \nMultiple days of the heat will lead to high heat risk west of the \nmountains, so make sure to be in areas of A/C and check on those \nwithout effective cooling. Models hint at the high becoming weaker \nby the end of next week, but just how weak it becomes is still \nuncertain. The forecast still has an outlook of temperatures above \nnormal, but please keep up with the latest forecast for additional \ndetails.",
